<p>Upon hearing of <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/26/movies/tim-curry-dead.html?unlocked_article_code=1.9VA.9xmj.NGnDUPkTnP9Z&amp;smid=url-share">Tim Curry’s death last week</a>, some of us could recall having seen him on stage: in <em>Amadeus </em>or&nbsp;<em>Spamalot</em> on Broadway, perhaps, though he also drew acclaim in&nbsp;the work of Stoppard, Brecht, and Shakespeare. For many more of us — perhaps all of us — it was his most memorable film and television performances that sprang to mind. To an extent, which ones are most memorable depends on the viewer. Some will forever remember Curry as DJ Johnny LaGuardia in&nbsp;<em>Times Square</em>,&nbsp;the deceitful Romanian philanthropist in <em>Congo</em>, the arch-nemesis King Chicken on <em>Duckman</em>, or even as the Bard himself in the series Will Shakespeare.</p>
<p>More will remember him as Dr. Frank-N-Furter, the cross-dressing mad scientist from the planet Transsexual at the center of the stage musical&nbsp;<em>The Rocky Horror Show</em> as well as its film adaptation <em>The Rocky Horror Picture Show</em>. The latter ended up becoming the much larger-scale <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2023/11/how-rocky-horror-became-a-cult-phenomenon.html">cultural phenomenon</a>, still screened at midnight&nbsp;to this day, more than half a century after its theatrical release.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>In that time, the audience-participation rituals have grown elaborate enough to overshadow the content. Still, if you really try to focus on the action onscreen, you’ll notice the remarkable extent to which — even now that all the tropes of&nbsp;science-fiction B movies, nineteen-fifties teen culture, and deliberate <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2026/08/the-history-of-glam-rock-how-it-put-an-end-to-the-hippie-era.html">glam-rock</a> excess have become barely recognizable —&nbsp;Curry’s performance remains vital.</p>
<p>Just six months ago, the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences put up <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P3VKRQr9Img">a video in which Curry looks back at his defining roles</a>. The list begins, naturally, with <em>Rocky Horror</em>, but also includes&nbsp;his turns as the Lord of Darkness in&nbsp;<em>Legend</em>&nbsp;(which he was offered after Ridley Scott saw him as Dr. Frank-N-Furter), Pennywise in&nbsp;<em>Stephen King’s It</em>&nbsp;(now regarded as the <em>ne plus ultra</em>&nbsp;of evil clown characters), and Long John Silver in&nbsp;<em>Muppet Treasure Island</em> (his personal favorite of his movies). We’re also told of parts he didn’t get, like Ian Malcolm in&nbsp;<em>Jurassic Park</em>, for which he was considered, or — more tantalizingly — Hannibal Lecter in&nbsp;<em>The Silence of the Lambs</em>, whom he “desperately wanted to play.” He also offhandedly mentions that, in addition to&nbsp;<em>Clue</em>’s three existing endings, he also shot a fourth in which his butler is revealed to have committed all the murders. “Of course, that was my favorite,” he says. Alas, “they never released that ending, because they thought it was too obvious.”</p>
